lisa-design:
- sudo stays in factory-agent's process group (the spec asked for its own group). Measured on the workstation (sudo 1.9.15p5, no tty and under a pty) and read in sudo's exec_nopty.c/exec_pty.c: sudo ignores a user-sent signal only when the sender's group is led by sudo or by its command, never factory-agent's; SIGTERM from factory-agent's own group was relayed and the helper exited. SIGKILL was the bug. An own group would also stop a terminal hangup's SIGHUP from reaching sudo (host init dies on SIGHUP without cancelling), orphaning the helper.
- Host: a `sudo -n` call (filepath.Base(name)=="sudo" && args[0]=="-n") gets Cancel=SIGTERM to sudo's pid and WaitDelay=helperGrace (30s); everything else, runRoot's `sudo /bin/sh` included, keeps CommandContext's SIGKILL and 10s.
- Helper: every verb under NotifyContext(SIGTERM, SIGINT, SIGHUP); ExecRunner puts a command in its own group and SIGKILLs the group on cancel, killWait 5s; a command given an *os.File stdin (only exec -i) stays in the helper's group, since a background group can't read a terminal.
- image-build takes a non-blocking flock on BuildDir for its whole run (a second fails "another image-build is running") and sweeps context-* under it, so it never sweeps a running build's context.
- Host-side image-build bound kataImageTimeout = 2h, beyond the helper's 90-minute buildTimeout so the helper's own bound normally reports.
